Consider a two-unit parallel repairable system in which unit 1 has an Erlang-2 (or generally Erlang-type) lifetime distribution with phase parameter a_1 > 0, and the system's state at time t is described by the following probability density functions on the indicated domains, with (x,u,t) ∈ [0,∞) × [0,∞) × [0,∞):

• p_{0,i}(x,t), i = 1,2: the joint density that at time t both units are operating, the operating unit 1 is in Erlang phase i, and the age of unit 2 is x;
• p_{1,i}(x,t), i = 1,2: the joint density that at time t unit 1 is operating in phase i and unit 2 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x;
• p_2(x,u,t): the joint density that at time t unit 2 is operating with age u and unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x;
• p_3(x,t): the density that at time t unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x and unit 2 is waiting for repair;
• p_4(x,u,t): the joint density that at time t unit 1 is being repaired with elapsed repair time x and unit 2 is in suspended repair with elapsed failure time u.

The rates are: a_1 > 0 (Erlang phase rate of unit 1), a_2(x) (repair rate of unit 1, depending on elapsed repair time x), b_1(x) (failure rate of unit 1's operating life, depending on age x), and b_2(x) (repair rate of unit 2). The dynamics are governed by a coupled system of first-order linear partial differential equations for the above densities, with integral boundary conditions of "supplementary-variable" type, including in particular
   p_{0,1}(0,t) = ∫_0^∞ p_{1,1}(x,t) b_2(x) dx + δ(t),
together with the analogous boundary equalities at x = 0 (and u = 0) for the remaining densities, and natural nonnegative initial data summing to 1. Here δ(·) is the Dirac delta function modelling the system starting in state (0,1) at t = 0. Assume the rate functions a_2, b_1, b_2 are nonnegative, measurable, and bounded.

Questions:

(a) Show that this PDE system is well-posed in a suitable Banach space setting; specifically, prove that it generates a positive C_0-semigroup of contractions and admits a unique nonnegative time-dependent solution whose total probability is conserved (equals 1) for every t ≥ 0.

(b) Determine the asymptotic behavior of the time-dependent solution as t → ∞: in particular, decide whether it converges (strongly, or exponentially) in the underlying Banach space to the steady-state solution corresponding to the eigenvalue 0 of the system's generator.

